{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' SYMBOLS.md; sed -n '1,240p' ERRORS.md; sed -n '1,260p' CONFIGS.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Dynamic Symbol Surface\n\nGenerated from the default C shared object with:\n\n```text\nnm -D --defined-only --format=posix c_src/build/libtranslated_rust.so\n```\n\nOnly defined global API symbols (`T`, `D`, `B`, or `R`) are included. Undefined\nGLIBC imports (`memset` and `sqrtf`) and toolchain weak symbols are not library\nAPI exports.\n\n| C symbol | kind | C declaration | Rust export | status |\n|----------|------|---------------|-------------|--------|\n| `normalize` | `T` | `void normalize(float *dest, const float *src, int size)` | `normalize` | [x] |\n\nMissing C symbols in the Rust shared object: **0**.\n\n# Error Surface\n\nThe mechanical scan covered `c_src/include/lib.h` and `c_src/src/lib.c` for\nerror-return statements/macros, `assert`, null checks, range checks, enums, and\nmin/max constants. The C API has one `void` function and contains none of those\nconstructs, so there are **zero source-derived rejection rows**.\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |\n|---|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n\n## Generic FFI Boundaries\n\nThese are required boundary probes rather than explicit C rejection branches.\nSignal results describe the built default C shared object; dereferencing null\nor supplying a length beyond the allocation is outside the C API's defined\nmemory contract.\n\n| # | function | boundary | expected C result | status |\n|---|----------|----------|-------------------|--------|\n| B1 | `normalize` | `dest == src == NULL`, `size == 0` | returns normally; no memory access | [ ] |\n| B2 | `normalize` | `dest == src`, `size == -1` | returns normally; both loops and `memset` are skipped | [ ] |\n| B3 | `normalize` | `dest == src`, `size == INT_MIN` | returns normally; both loops and `memset` are skipped | [ ] |\n| B4 | `normalize` | null source with positive size | process-level fault from source dereference | [ ] |\n| B5 | `normalize` | null destination, nonzero source, positive size | process-level fault from destination write | [ ] |\n| B6 | `normalize` | distinct pointers with `size == -1` | process-level fault from the C conversion of `size * sizeof(float)` to a huge `memset` length | [ ] |\n| B7 | `normalize` | `size == INT_MAX` with null buffers | process-level fault on the first source access; covers the oversized-length boundary | [ ] |\n\nThere are no documented numeric ranges or enum parameters, so no one-past-range\nor invalid-enum case exists for this API.\n\n# Configuration Surface\n\n## Build-Time Configurations\n\n`Cargo.toml` has no `[features]` table, and `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` has no CMake\noptions, conditional sources, or compile definitions. The complete feature\nmatrix therefore contains one configuration:\n\n| # | Cargo features | C configuration | check command | status |\n|---|----------------|-----------------|---------------|--------|\n| F1 | none (`--no-default-features`) | default CMake configuration | `cargo check --no-default-features --features \"\"` | [x] |\n\n## Runtime Configurations\n\nThe public surface has no runtime option or mode setters and one entry point:\n`normalize`. Rows below derive from the two C conditions (`sum > 0.0f` and\n`dest != src`), the two loops, IEEE-754 values that select each condition, and\nthe pointer layouts observable through the public API.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | status |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|--------|\n| C1 | `normalize` | `size == 0`, distinct valid pointers; zero-iteration loops and zero-byte `memset` | [ ] |\n| C2 | `normalize` | `size == 0`, `dest == src`; zero-iteration loops and skipped `memset` | [ ] |\n| C3 | `normalize` | `size == 1`, finite value with positive square, distinct buffers | [ ] |\n| C4 | `normalize` | `size > 1`, finite values with finite positive sum, distinct buffers | [ ] |\n| C5 | `normalize` | `size > 1`, finite positive sum, in-place (`dest == src`) | [ ] |\n| C6 | `normalize` | finite positive sum, forward overlap (`dest == src + 1`) | [ ] |\n| C7 | `normalize` | finite positive sum, backward overlap (`src == dest + 1`) | [ ] |\n| C8 | `normalize` | many signed zero values, distinct buffers; `sum == 0`, destination zero-filled | [ ] |\n| C9 | `normalize` | many signed zero values, in-place; `sum == 0`, destination left unchanged | [ ] |\n| C10 | `normalize` | nonzero tiny values whose squared sum underflows to zero, distinct buffers | [ ] |\n| C11 | `normalize` | nonzero tiny values whose squared sum underflows to zero, in-place | [ ] |\n| C12 | `normalize` | input containing NaN, distinct buffers; comparison is false and destination is zero-filled | [ ] |\n| C13 | `normalize` | input containing NaN, in-place; comparison is false and input is unchanged | [ ] |\n| C14 | `normalize` | finite values whose squared sum overflows to positive infinity | [ ] |\n| C15 | `normalize` | input containing positive or negative infinity; positive-sum branch with zero reciprocal | [ ] |\n| C16 | `normalize` | `sum <= 0` path with partially overlapping distinct pointers; destination range is zero-filled | [ ] |\n| C17 | `normalize` | large valid vectors (4096 elements), finite positive sum | [ ] |\n| C18 | `normalize` | mixed signs and magnitudes with randomized finite bit patterns and finite positive sum | [ ] |\n| C19 | `normalize` | positive sum with an output buffer initialized to arbitrary bytes | [ ] |\n| C20 | `normalize` | zero/NaN sum with an output buffer initialized to arbitrary bytes | [ ] |\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
